# The ER Model and Normalisation: Designing Good Schemas

> Databases outlive the applications built on them, so their schemas
> must be right the first time. This lesson moves from ER sketches —
> entities, attributes, relationships — to fully normalised tables,
> watching one messy table shrink into four clean ones.

## 1. What Schema Design Is For

A schema is the permanent grammar of a database. Redesigning code is
cheap; redesigning a live schema means migrating billions of rows
while the business keeps running. Good design therefore aims at three
targets:

- **No redundant facts** — every fact stored once, so no copy can go
  stale.
- **No impossible data** — constraints make wrong data unrepresentable.
- **Room to grow** — new attributes and rules slot in without breaking
  existing tables.

The classic pipeline: gather requirements, draw an **ER diagram**,
convert it to relational tables, then **normalise** the result to
remove redundancy the sketch missed.

## 2. Entities, Attributes, and Relationships

[definition]
The **Entity-Relationship (ER) model** describes a business domain as
*entities* (things worth recording, drawn as rectangles), *attributes*
(properties, drawn as ovals), and *relationships* (associations
between entities, drawn as diamonds).
[/definition]

@diagram tree id="er-building-blocks" layout="tree" aria="Building blocks of the ER model"
  "ER model" -> Entity
  "ER model" -> Attribute
  "ER model" -> Relationship
  Entity -> "Strong: has its own key"
  Entity -> "Weak: borrows its owner's key"
  Attribute -> "Simple: city"
  Attribute -> "Composite: name into first and last"
  Attribute -> "Multivalued: phone numbers"
  Attribute -> "Derived: age from date of birth"
  Relationship -> "One-to-one"
  Relationship -> "One-to-many"
  Relationship -> "Many-to-many"
@/diagram

For a university: STUDENT and COURSE are entities; name, city, and
credits are attributes; *enrols in* is the relationship that connects
them, carrying its own attribute — the grade. A relationship can have
data of its own, and that observation later justifies a separate
table.

## 3. Cardinality and Participation

Cardinality says how many of each side may participate:

| Type | Reading | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1:1 | one of each, paired | person — passport |
| 1:N | one parent, many children | department — employees |
| M:N | many on both sides | students — courses |

- **Total participation** — every instance must take part (every
  enrolment names a student): drawn with a double line.
- **Partial participation** — some instances may sit out (a student
  may take no course this term).

M:N is the interesting case: a student takes many courses, a course
holds many students. Relational tables have no native M:N construct,
so the relationship itself becomes a table.

## 4. From ER Diagram to Tables

| ER construct | Relational mapping |
| --- | --- |
| Strong entity | its own table, key becomes primary key |
| Weak entity | table including the owner's key in its own |
| Composite attribute | flatten into plain columns |
| Multivalued attribute | separate table with the owner's key |
| 1:N relationship | foreign key on the N side pointing at the 1 side |
| 1:1 relationship | foreign key on either side, or merge the tables |
| M:N relationship | junction table with both keys (plus its own attributes) |

Applying the rules to the university sketch gives exactly three
tables: STUDENT(student_id, name, city), COURSE(course_id, title,
credits), and the junction ENROLMENT(student_id, course_id, grade) —
with (student_id, course_id) as a composite primary key and grade as
the relationship's own attribute.

## 5. The Price of One Big Table

Suppose instead we flatten everything into one wide,
everything-in-one-place table:

**ENROLMENT_REPORT(student_id, name, city, course_id, title, credits,
grade)** — key (student_id, course_id):

| student_id | name | city | course_id | title | credits | grade |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Aisha | Delhi | C1 | Databases | 4 | A |
| 1 | Aisha | Delhi | C2 | Networks | 3 | B |
| 2 | Boris | Mumbai | C1 | Databases | 4 | B |
| 3 | Chen | Delhi | C1 | Databases | 4 | A |

Aisha's name and city repeat on every row; Databases' title and
credits repeat for every enroled student. Redundancy invites the
three classic **anomalies**:

- **Update anomaly** — Aisha moves to Chennai; every row mentioning
  her must be edited. Miss one and the database disagrees with itself.
- **Insertion anomaly** — a new course, Compilers, cannot be recorded
  until some student enrols, because its row would have no student
  part of the key.
- **Deletion anomaly** — delete the last enrolment in Networks and the
  course's title and credits vanish from the database entirely.

[definition]
An **anomaly** is an unwanted side effect of a redundant design: an
update that must touch many rows, a fact that cannot be inserted, or a
fact that disappears when an unrelated row is deleted.
[/definition]

Normalisation is a mechanical cure — follow the decision path:

@diagram flowchart id="split-or-keep" layout="hierarchical" aria="Deciding how to split a table while normalising"
node start label="One wide table" shape="pill"
node q1 label="Non-atomic or repeating values?" shape="diamond"
node a1 label="Flatten to 1NF" shape="round-rect"
node q2 label="Fact depends on part of the key?" shape="diamond"
node a2 label="Split to 2NF" shape="round-rect"
node q3 label="Fact depends on another non-key fact?" shape="diamond"
node a3 label="Split to 3NF" shape="round-rect"
node done label="Clean schema" shape="round-rect"

start -> q1
q1 -> a1 label="yes"
a1 -> q2
q1 -> q2 label="no"
q2 -> a2 label="yes"
a2 -> q3
q2 -> q3 label="no"
q3 -> a3 label="yes"
a3 -> done
q3 -> done label="no"
@/diagram

## 6. Functional Dependencies: The Algebra of Keys

[definition]
A **functional dependency (FD)** X → Y holds when the value of X
determines the value of Y: any two rows agreeing on X must agree on Y.
In ENROLMENT_REPORT: student_id → name, course_id → credits,
(student_id, course_id) → grade.
[/definition]

FDs are discovered from the business, not from the data — if the
college reuses a course_id for two titles, that is a bug, not a new
dependency. Two FD facts matter for design:

- A **key** is a column set that functionally determines *every*
  attribute of the table.
- FDs compose by **transitivity**: from student_id → hostel_id and
  hostel_id → hostel_name follows student_id → hostel_name, even
  though no rule said so directly.

## 7. 1NF, 2NF, 3NF: A Worked Normalisation

The normal forms are a ladder; each rung removes one redundancy
pattern:

@diagram tree id="normal-forms-ladder" layout="tree" aria="Progression of normal forms"
  "Unnormalised" -> "1NF: atomic values only"
  "1NF: atomic values only" -> "2NF: no partial dependencies"
  "2NF: no partial dependencies" -> "3NF: no transitive dependencies"
  "3NF: no transitive dependencies" -> "BCNF: every determinant is a key"
@/diagram

**1NF — atomic values.** A STUDENT row holding `phones: 98110, 99201`
stores two facts in one cell; queries like "find students with phone
98110" become string surgery. Fix: one fact per cell — move each phone
to its own row of a PHONE(student_id, phone) table. Our wide table is
already in 1NF.

**2NF — the whole key.** 1NF plus: no non-key attribute may depend on
a *part* of a composite key. Here name and city depend on student_id
alone, title and credits on course_id alone — partial dependencies.
Split along the determinants:

- STUDENT(student_id, name, city, hostel_id, hostel_name)
- COURSE(course_id, title, credits)
- ENROLMENT(student_id, course_id, grade) — key (student_id, course_id)

**3NF — nothing but the key.** 2NF plus: no non-key attribute may
depend on another non-key attribute (no *transitive* dependency).
STUDENT still hides one: student_id → hostel_id → hostel_name. Split:

- HOSTEL(hostel_id, hostel_name)
- STUDENT(student_id, name, city, hostel_id)

[example]
Trace one fact through the ladder: "Databases is worth 4 credits"
appeared three times in ENROLMENT_REPORT, once per enrolled student.
After 2NF it appears once, in COURSE. Updating credits is now one
row write — the update anomaly is gone by construction.
[/example]

[tip]
The classic exam mnemonic: every attribute must depend on *the key
(1NF), the whole key (2NF), and nothing but the key (3NF) — so help
me Codd*. Map each clause to a dependency type and the normal form
follows.
[/tip]

## 8. BCNF, Trade-offs, and When to Denormalise

**BCNF** tightens 3NF: for *every* non-trivial FD X → Y, X must be a
superkey — even when X is itself a non-key column set. 3NF tables can
rarely still hide a dependency whose determinant is not a key; BCNF
forbids those. Decomposition to BCNF may sacrifice dependency
preservation, which is why 3NF is a common stopping point for
exam-style designs.

Normalisation costs joins: reconstructing the report in section 5 now
means joining four tables. For most workloads the optimiser handles
that easily, but two situations justify deliberate **denormalisation**
— storing some redundancy on purpose:

| Factor | Normalised | Denormalised |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Write correctness | anomalies impossible | application must keep copies in step |
| Read joins | more joins, more work | fewer joins, faster simple reads |
| Storage | minimal | redundant bytes grow with row count |
| Best fit | transactional systems (OLTP) | reporting shelves, read-heavy caches |

The storage bill for redundancy grows linearly with usage:

@chart bar title="Redundant bytes stored for 100,000 enrolments (MB)" labels:
One big table (name, city, title, credits repeated)
Normalised (each fact once)

data:
5.2
0.05
@/chart

[warning]
Denormalise only when a measured, read-heavy workload proves the need
— and let the application, not luck, keep the duplicated columns in
step. Doing it "for speed" without measurements is how stale-data bugs
are born.
[/warning]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recap |
| --- | --- |
| ER model | entities, attributes, relationships; M:N needs a junction table |
| Mapping | 1:N puts the FK on the N side; M:N becomes its own table |
| Anomalies | update, insertion, deletion — all children of redundancy |
| FD | X → Y: X determines Y; keys determine everything |
| 1NF | atomic values, no repeating groups |
| 2NF | no non-key attribute depends on part of a composite key |
| 3NF | no non-key attribute depends on another non-key |
| BCNF | every determinant is a superkey |
| Denormalisation | deliberate redundancy for reads; guard the writes |

[summary]
ER modelling captures the domain as entities, attributes, and
relationships, and mechanical rules turn the diagram into tables —
with M:N relationships becoming junction tables. One wide table
demonstrates the update, insertion, and deletion anomalies; functional
dependencies diagnose them, and 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF remove them step by
step until every fact depends on the key, the whole key, and nothing
but the key. Denormalise only on measured evidence.
[/summary]
